  {
    q: 'What is the salary of a logistics professional in India?',
    a: 'Entry-level operations executives (0-2 years) typically earn Rs 2.5-5 LPA. Supply chain or planning analysts (2-5 years) with genuine data and analytics fluency earn Rs 6-12 LPA. Logistics or operations managers (5-10 years) with real P&L or vendor-network ownership earn Rs 12-22 LPA, and senior supply chain leaders (10+ years) at large companies can reach Rs 25-45 LPA and above. Specialised niches like international freight forwarding or customs compliance can pay Rs 5-15 LPA depending on experience.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will automation replace logistics jobs?',
    a: 'Not the field as a whole, but automation is genuinely reshaping which roles stay valuable. Warehouse robotics, AI-driven route planning, and automated tracking are reducing demand for the most repetitive physical and manual-scheduling work, and basic data-entry-style tracking roles are shrinking. At the same time, India\'s logistics sector overall is growing with e-commerce and manufacturing expansion, and demand for people who can manage vendor networks, handle genuine disruptions, and make strategic supply chain decisions is rising, not falling.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need an MBA to succeed in logistics or supply chain management?',
    a: 'Not to enter the field, but an MBA or postgraduate supply chain management qualification meaningfully accelerates the path into planning and leadership roles, especially when layered on top of genuine operational experience rather than in place of it. Many successful supply chain leaders start in operational roles and build toward management through demonstrated skill and, often but not always, a later qualification.',
  },
