        <tr>
          <th>Stage</th>
          <th>Typical range</th>
          <th>Reality</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {salaryRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Stage"><strong>{item.stage}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Typical range">{item.range}</td>
            <td data-label="Reality">{item.reality}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Ranges are directional, based on aggregated 2025-2026 salary-tracking data at the time of writing. Verify
    current figures against live listings for your specific city, sector, and company type before making a
    financial decision.
  </p>

  <h2 id="automation-risk">The honest automation question</h2>

  <p>
    This is the question every "is logistics a good career" search is really circling in 2026, and it deserves a
    specific, evidence-based answer rather than a reflexive yes or no.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What automation is genuinely replacing</div>
      <ul>
        {automationComparison.gold.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Why the sector overall keeps growing</div>
      <ul>
        {automationComparison.teal.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      The clearest way to think about this: automation is compressing the routine, predictable parts of logistics
      work, which raises the bar for what counts as a differentiated, hireable skill set. Someone who only performs
      repetitive manual tracking or scheduling is competing directly against increasingly capable automated
      systems. Someone who can manage vendor relationships, handle genuine disruptions, and work with planning data
      is more valuable than before, not less.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="growth-scaling">How senior logistics professionals actually scale their income</h2>

  <p>
    Logistics can plateau exactly like any other job — staying in execution-only roles indefinitely with no added
    specialisation has a real, moderate ceiling. But there is genuine headroom for people who move deliberately.
  </p>

  <div class="employer-grid">
